    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Periods can now be closed, and all 4 tests in the `bin/e2e` acceptance spec pass.\n\n**What a user sees**\n- Each period row shows closed `yes`/`no` and has a Close button. If closing fails, an error appears on that row (for example, when the period is already closed).\n- Once a period is closed, its due-total is fixed at the amount it had when it closed. Later rate changes don't alter it. Each claim's own due and the people totals still follow the current rates; the spec checks that it works this way.\n- A claim dated inside a closed period can't be added, edited, opened, settled or voided. Each attempt shows an error on the form or claim row and changes nothing. An edit that would move a claim's date into a closed period is refused too.\n\n**What changed**\n- **Stored data:** new migration `V15__period_closed.sql` adds a `closed` flag and the fixed total to each period.\n- **Server:**\n  - Closing is a new `POST \u2026/periods/{id}/close` request. It calculates the period's due-total the same way the screen already did and writes `PERIOD_CLOSED \u2026 period=<name> total=<due-total>` to the audit file.\n  - `ClaimService` checks for a closed period before any claim change. It also locks the sheet first, so a period can't close partway through a claim change.\n- **Front end:** the periods table has the new column, button and error. A closed period shows its fixed total, and both claim error messages explain when a claim is in a closed period.\n\n**Decisions you may want to revisit**\n- Every claim change now locks the sheet, so changes to the same sheet happen one at a time. That's simple and safe, but it could slow down busy sheets.\n- A refused claim change returns HTTP 423 (Locked) so the screen can show a specific message.\n- There's no way to reopen a period, because the request didn't ask for one.",
